# Form named controls shadow the whole prototype chain
#
# Patch 297 made a form's named controls shadow the form's own
# interface, as HTMLFormElement's [LegacyOverrideBuiltIns] does in
# browsers, by wrapping HTMLFormElement.prototype's own members in
# place and a hand-listed set of the form's IDL attributes that the
# bridge keeps on the shared element prototype above it. The wider
# chain was left unwrapped, so a control named after an HTMLElement,
# Element or Node member -- id, title, children, focus, tagName,
# nodeType, className -- did not shadow: form.id answered with the
# form's own id reflection and the control was only reachable
# through form.elements. Browsers shadow the whole chain.
#
# Pinned against Firefox before fixing (2026-10-19): on a form
# carrying controls named id, title, children, focus, tagName,
# nodeType and className, plus one with id "dir", every one of
# those reads is the control, by name and by id; a form without
# collisions reads its own values (id 'plain', title, tagName
# 'FORM', nodeType 1); and a write to a shadowed accessor still
# reaches the built-in setter -- form.id = 'changed' sets the
# form's id attribute -- while reads keep returning the control.
#
# The wrapping therefore walks the whole chain: every own member
# of every prototype from HTMLFormElement.prototype up to (but not
# including) Object.prototype is wrapped where it sits, the lowest
# occurrence of each name deciding (a member found lower is the one
# reads resolve to, so a higher duplicate is never wrapped). The
# hand-list is gone; the walk finds those members at their homes.
# The accessor setter no longer swallows a write to a shadowed
# name, matching the pin: writes reach the built-in, reads stay
# control-first. (Patch 297's method writes are unchanged: a write
# to a shadowed method name still does not stick, and its gate
# check reads the control back.) The form-only guard is unchanged,
# so every other element's reads and writes behave exactly as
# before, and the reentrancy guard still keeps the control
# search's own reads out of the wrapping.
#
# Deliberate remainders, recorded: Object.prototype's members are
# not wrapped -- putting a wrapped getter in front of every object
# in the page is too broad a hop for the sake of a control named
# toString, which keeps not shadowing. A name whose lowest member
# is not configurable cannot be wrapped and keeps not shadowing
# (the wrap-in-place architecture's standing limit, as in 297).
# Members defined on the chain after the polyfill's named-access
# setup runs would not be wrapped either; none are today.
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-form-shadow-chain.html (FSHADOW2 10/10
# in the simulator and in a reference browser -- Firefox/Camoufox,
# for the reason patch 292's header gives; the page scored 1/10
# before the fix, only its no-collision check passing).
# JS-form-shadow.html stays FORMSHADOW 6/6 and JS-form-named.html
# stays FORMNAMED 7/7. Simulator only; the vita cross-build was
# not run (no vitaSDK on this VM). Device expectation is in
# Doc 10.
#
# Decline conditions: as patch 297's -- a real named-properties
# object in the bridge replaces this wrapping wholesale.
